  "id": "Compound::DB00961 - Gene::6328",
  "verbalization": "Mepivacaine, a local anesthetic, is linked to Scn3a through a series of molecular pathways that involve amide-based compounds. The evidence suggests that Mepivacaine, as an amide, is included in the class of compounds that include Prilocaine and Ropivacaine, both of which bind to Scn3a. This binding is consistent with the mechanism of action of local anesthetics, which typically inhibit sodium channels, including the Scn3a channel, thereby reducing pain perception. The shared ancestry of Mepivacaine with Prilocaine and Ropivacaine, along with their common inclusion in the amide class, supports a mechanistic link between these compounds and Scn3a. The final scores from the paths indicate a strong, consistent relationship, suggesting that the observed interactions are biologically relevant. The evidence is supported by the presence of a lowest common ancestor in the amide class, reinforcing the idea that the interaction between Mepivacaine and Scn3a is rooted in a shared chemical structure and biological function.",
